 | E-201 | **A Phase 1 battery spent one instruction screen per item, and metric-coverage pressure rather than burst eligibility was the cause. No per-type response-time data exists anywhere in this repository to price the trade in seconds.** An instruction screen is a served item whose type differs from the item before it, so it counts the times a child must read how to answer. Measured over 8 born-synthetic probabilistic children spanning the ability range in all four areas, grade band `4-5`, 40-item cap, selecting over the served INDEX with the app's own `EXAM_ENGINE_OVERRIDES` — not the engine defaults and not the research catalog's measurement lists, whose substitution is itself the reason bursting was demonstrated in simulation while never firing in a browser. **`dev` as shipped: 26.9 items, 26.9 instruction screens, 100% of items opening one.** Three independent causes, each individually sufficient: (1) the served index sets `content` to `{}`, so no item anywhere looked like a bounded choice and **0 of 48 types were burstable** whatever else was true; (2) the eligibility rule disqualified any type declaring `M-PATH`/`M-EFF`/`M-PLANFUL`/`M-IDEAFLU`; (3) `nextType` summed tracked-inert metric shortfalls without limit, making the type in each area that declares the most process measurements worth 4 points against its rivals' 2 — more than the 1.5-point age-band preference and 40× the tie-break jitter — so it won every selection in its area until its counts filled. That is why the child met exactly six types (`QUANT-MIX-01`, `SPA-XPLANE-01`, `GB-WORDFORGE-01`, `CX-check-01`, `GB-EXPLORE-01`, `VER-SEQUENCE-01`), five of them constructed-response with **zero** of their items offering an option list, so never burstable under any rule. **Arm-by-arm attribution, and it inverts the expected diagnosis: 19 types were ALREADY eligible and simply never selected.** Option count on the index alone: 25.4 screens (−6%) with the session 8% LONGER (29.1 items). Plus retiring the