tanding assertions in `packages/exam-engine/src/burst.test.ts` and `apps/web/src/lib/exam/adaptive.test.ts`, the latter driving `planNextSelection` over the served index so the loop the runner runs is the loop under test | An instruction screen is COUNTED, never priced: if a screen is cheap relative to a question then 42% fewer screens is a smaller improvement than it reads, and if construct variety matters more than the owner's stated objective then the 4.3 → 3.2 narrowing is a loss rather than a cost. Burst eligibility claims only that consecutive items are each a single unpaced choice; if answering one of them is in fact slow, a burst is merely less beneficial, not harmful. Nothing here bears on score validity, reliability or fairness | Measurement owner | **Open — implemented, awaiting owner sign-off (D-201). Which types are "fast" remains unmeasured; the collection that would settle it is specified in `EXAM_BURST_INSTRUCTION_COST.md` §5 and needs one persisted session plus one query, no code change.** |
 | E-202 | **Every Phase 1 sitting served the same question types in the same order, and the fixed seed was only one of three causes.** `DEFAULT_CONFIG.seed` was the constant `0xc0ffee` and nothing overrode it, so every selection draw was identical in every sitting; the type choice was a strict argmax whose only variation was a ±0.1 jitter, too small to outweigh a metric weight (1 or 2) or the age bonus (1.5); and nothing discouraged returning to a type just served. Measured over 20 differently-seeded sittings on the REAL wired bank with the ENGINE defaults and the research catalog's measurement lists: **6 of 49 distinct types reached before, 23 after; worst within-session repeat over 20 items 5.0 → 3.1.** On the wired bank the order was literally a four-type cycle (`VER-POLYSEME-01 > FLU-LADDER-01 > SPA-XPLANE-01 > QUANT-GRAPH-01 > ...`). **Attribution:** the seed alone fixes variety BETWEEN children and leaves within-session repetition completely unchanged (5.0 → 5.0); all three parts were needed. **Two constraints found by measurement rather than reasoning, both pinned by tests.** `itemSelectionTolerance` must stay strictly below `ageBandBias`: a non-matching item is charged exactly `ageBandBias`, so a tolerance of 0.5 makes a band-matching and a non-matching item at the same difficulty interchangeable and silently cancels D-025; set to 0.25. The recency discount must not apply while an area is still homing: applied throughout it cost a distant-seeded child 4 items and broke D-023's convergence budget (52 against 48), while after the estimate straddles the same discount is free. | Verified measurement of routing behaviour over born-synthetic uncalibrated banks (`validated=false`) | `packages/exam-engine/src/selection-variety.test.ts` over `loadRealBanks()`; decision D-202. Superseded in part by E-203, which re-measures every figure here against the configuration a browser actually runs and finds the fixed-rotation diagnosis does NOT reproduce there | If a child's