 | E-203 | **Capping tracked-inert coverage (D-201) and adding exposure control (D-202) are COMPLEMENTARY rather than mutually cancelling, but only after the type tolerance is re-derived against the capped scores, and the combination gives back half of D-201's stop-rule reliability.** Four arms at matched settings — same abilities, responder seeds, engine seeds, grade band and 40-item cap — over 80 differently-seeded sittings on the REAL wired bank, selecting over the served INDEX with the app's own `EXAM_ENGINE_OVERRIDES`, i.e. the configuration a child actually sits. **Instruction screens per session: `dev` 28.6, D-201 alone 18.5, D-202 alone 28.0, combined 19.7.** **Distinct types per session: `dev` 18.1, D-201 alone 14.9, D-202 alone 19.8, combined 17.1** — so D-201 alone costs 3.2 types per session and adding D-202 recovers 2.2 of them, at a cost of 1.2 instruction screens. **Worst return-to-type over 20 items (a burst of four counted as one arrival): `dev` 3.4, D-201 alone 1.5, D-202 alone 3.1, combined 1.4** — the lowest of the four arms. **Enforced metric coverage completed in 80 of 80 sittings in every arm.** **The residual cost: 15 of 80 sittings reach the 40-item safety cap in the combination, against 10 for D-201 alone and 17 for `dev`** — so the combination is better than `dev` and worse than D-201 alone on the signature of a battery becoming fixed-length. **The retune, and its mechanism.** `typeSelectionTolerance` was derived at 1.0 against the UNCAPPED coverage sum, where a type could earn four tracked-metric points; capping the tracked total at one point compressed the score spread, making the same tolerance proportionally a much wider net. At 1.0 exactly, a type holding a tracked shortfall and one holding none fall within tolerance of each other, and a type closing an ENFORCED shortfall (weight 2) falls within tolerance of a rival closing none but holding the full capped tracked gain (2 − 1 = 1.0). Swept over 80 sittings: **1.0 costs 1.9 items per session against 0.5 and buys 0.3 of a distinct type**, so the tolerance moved to 0.5 and the constraint is now stated as `typeSelectionTolerance < trackedCoverageCap`. Below about 1/3 the knob does nothing at all, because no score gap is narrower than the smallest recency step. `typeRecencyPenalty` 1.0, `typeRecencyWindow` 3 and `itemSelectionTolerance` 0.25 were swept on the same grid and are at their measured optimum; they are unchanged. **E-202's fixed-rotation diagnosis does not reproduce under the browser configuration.** With a per-session seed, `dev`'s own rule already reaches 47 of 48 wired types across 20 sittings there, because the generated registry gives types much flatter metric lists than the research catalog does and the argmax therefore ties far more often. E-202's 6-of-49 is a fact about the ENGINE defaults. What a cohort meets today, on the constant seed actually shipped, is 24 distinct types across 20 sittings. **A pinned constraint went slack.** D-202's homing guard was held by D-023's convergence budget; with the coverage cap in place the worst battery runs 47 items against a budget of 48 whether the guard is present or removed, so that test would no longer catch its removal. Re-pinned directly on the served type order, and the new assertions were verified to fail when the guard is removed. The `itemSelectionTolerance < ageBandBias` pin was verified to still fail when the tolerance is raised to 0.5. | Verified measurement of routing behaviour over born-synthetic probabilistic children and uncalibrated banks (`validated=false`) | `pnpm exam:selection-integration` (`scripts/exam-selection-integration.ts`, `--sweep` for the knob grid, `--sessions=N` for the cohort size) reproduces every figure; method and claim boundaries in `docs/product/EXAM_SELECTION_INTEGRATION.md`; decision D-203. Shares `scripts/exam-selection-harness.ts` with `pnpm exam:instruction-cost`, so the two measurements cannot drift apart | If the residual cap pressure matters more than construct breadth, the combination is worse than D-201 alone and the tolerances should go to zero. Instruction screens are COUNTED, never priced in seconds — no per-type response-time data exists in this repository (E-201). Nothing here bears on score validity, reliability or fairness | Measurement owner | **Open — implemented, awaiting owner sign-off (D-203), which inherits the sign-off D-201 and D-202 are each still awaiting. The 40-item cap pressure the combination adds is unresolved: 15 of 80 against 10** |
 | E-204 | **Stage 1 selection never read its own uncertainty, and the standing level it reported was about a point and a half above the child. Correcting the chance-success floor and selecting on a live belief fixes the bias and what the child is handed, and costs a little precision per item.** Four arms at matched settings — same abilities, responder seeds, engine seeds, grade band and 40-item cap — over 60 differently-seeded probabilistic sittings on the REAL wired bank under the app's own `EXAM_ENGINE_OVERRIDES`. **`staircase` (as shipped) against `mepv` (proposed): reported standing bias +1.577 → +0.017 and RMSE 2.352 → 1.250; refitted ability RMSE 2.047 → 1.300; sessions concluding on evidence 52/60 → 58/60 with safety-cap hits 12 → 2; distinct types per session 17.6 → 18.0 and per area 4.39 → 4.50.** **What the child is handed, which is the BrainLift's actual claim and was never previously measured: items at least 3 scale points above them 21.4% → 7.6%, and the worst run of consecutive misses within an area 4.08 → 2.22.** **The cost is precision per item, in the opposite direction to the intuition that motivated the change:** mean final SE 1.235 → 1.286, items to an SE of 1.50 5.1 → 5.6, to 1.25 6.6 → 7.0, and areas ever reaching 1.25 159/240 → 147/240. The corrected aim deliberately serves items EASIER than the child — with a floor `c`, information peaks at `P = (1+sqrt(1+8c))/4`, about 0.653 for a five-option item, roughly 0.27 scale points below ability (Birnbaum 1968; Lord 1980; Magis 2013) — so the gain is accuracy, not speed to a stated precision. **`mfi` (maximum information at the belief mean) was measured, not assumed away, and it wins the obvious metric while losing the one that matters:** 6.4 items to an SE of 1.25 against `mepv`'s 7.0, 195/240 areas reaching it against 147, mean SE 1.138 against 1.286 — but refitted RMSE 1.740 against 1.300, so it reports ±1.14 while erring ±1.74 where `mepv` reports 1.29 and errs 1.30. **The SE comparison is partly circular in `mfi`'s favour and is reported as such:** `mfi` maximises information at the estimate and the SE is one over the root of exactly that information, so scoring the two criteria by SE scores `mfi` on its own objective, while RMSE against a planted ability favours neither by construction. This is a partial reproduction of Chen/Ankenmann/Chang and Choi/Swartz — elaborate criteria add little on PRECISION — and a non-reproduction on accuracy. **`cut` (maximum information at a decision point) reproduces Spray & Reckase and is not adopted:** against an ILLUSTRATIVE cut of 15 (no real GT threshold exists in this repository and none was invented) it reaches a 95% above/below call in 2.3 items across 223/240 areas, against `mepv`'s 3.0 across 183/240 — and pays with an ability estimate worthless away from the cut (refitted RMSE 3.403, mean SE 3.199, area SE spread 4.038 against 0.528), with the child's experience (47.8% of items at least 3 points above them, worst miss run 5.42), and with exposure control: **265 distinct bank items against 1,329**, the most-served item reaching 28% of sittings against 13%. **Two negative results worth as much as the positive ones.** Capping the ENFORCED coverage sum so diversity could outrank it changes nothing at all — identical results at caps of 2, 4, 6 and none — because no wired type ever earns more than one enforced gap's weight, so the enforced sum D-201 deliberately left free was never the binding constraint on diversity; the knob was reverted rather than shipped inert. And `areaSpreadSlack` of 1 is inert under the portal's `evenSpreadTolerance` of 1 while changing behaviour under the engine's own defaults, where it caused a within-session type-repeat regression; it ships at 0, so uncertainty breaks ties among the least-served areas and even spread is unchanged by construction. **The floor is harmful when wrong in either direction and is pinned in both.** Against a guessing child, assuming none inflates the reported level (+1.32 at floor 0, +0.35 at 0.1, +0.12 at 0.2, −0.06 at 0.25, −0.46 at 0.35); against a child who does NOT guess, assuming one attenuates them, and for a FIXED trace a floor can only move an estimate down, since `P(wrong) = (1−c)(1−s)` and the `(1−c)` cancels in the likelihood ratio — a miss carries identical information under any floor while a pass carries less. | Verified measurement of routing and RECOVERY behaviour over born-synthetic probabilistic children and uncalibrated banks (`syntheticOnly = true`, `validated = false`) | `pnpm exam:selection-diversity` (`scripts/exam-selection-diversity.ts`; `--sessions=N`, `--floor-sweep`, `--knob-sweep`) reproduces every figure, sharing `scripts/exam-selection-harness.ts` with `exam:instruction-cost` and `exam:selection-integration` so the three cannot drift apart. Standing assertions: `packages/exam-engine/src/posterior.test.ts` (the information peak against the closed form, the refutation of endpoint targeting as arithmetic, and that a belief will not claim a scale bound from a one-sided trace), `real-bank.test.ts` (`the chance-success floor, in both directions` — a matched pair, so the guard cannot pass vacuously), `packages/exam-scoring/src/ability.test.ts` (the same pair on the reporting fit, plus that omitting the knob reproduces the previous fit exactly). Decision D-204. Builds on `docs/architecture/ITEM_SELECTION_BRACKETING_ANALYSIS.md`, whose +1.44 bias estimate this reproduces and corrects | The 0.2 floor, the slope of 1.0 and the prior SD of 6.0 are DESIGN ASSUMPTIONS on an uncalibrated born-synthetic bank, not calibrated parameters; an estimated `c` is typically below the reciprocal of the option count, so 0.653 is an upper anchor rather than a constant, and the wired banks mix four-, five- and six-option items. Recovery under a simulated responder is not evidence about score validity, reliability, fairness or giftedness — it says the machinery can read back an ability the trace was generated from, and nothing more. The `cut` figures are shaped entirely by an illustrative threshold and must never be read as how a real gate would perform. Every standing level already shown to anyone moves downward under this change | Measurement owner | **Supported — implemented, and D-204 approved by the owner 2026-07-31. The product question this measurement was meant to inform is now settled: Stage 1 is a LOCATOR, not a gate — a per-area standing level that scales Stage 2, where precision away from any cut is the product (D-205, decided on arm four of this measurement). `cut` selection remains a supported policy value carrying the price recorded above, so a later decision to run a gate is a configuration change rather than a rebuild.** |
+| E-205 | **The learning-rate residual E-200 left behind is endogeneity of the adaptive design, not a defect of the fit, and no change to the fit removes it. Breaking the loop does remove it, and costs more reportability than it buys.** E-200 corrected the chance floor and left λ̄ ≈ +0.009 for a cohort that learned nothing. Three banks across three domains and an idealised grid containing no items at all return that same value, so it is not a bank property; the shipped default already matches the five-option format, so it is not the floor either. **The cause, settled by one contrast rather than by argument.** Replay each child's OWN adaptive difficulty path a second time against an independent stream of answers — identical path, identical ability, identical standing handover, only the dependence between the answers and the path cut — and the manufactured climb goes: **+0.0085 ± 0.0013 becomes −0.0039 ± 0.0010 on the bank-free grid at an unchanged difficulty climb of 0.29 scale points**, and +0.0097 → −0.0035 on `FLU-OPCHAIN-01` at an unchanged 0.35. **A rising ladder is therefore innocent, and a steeper one manufactures less: an exogenous schedule climbing 1.63 points across the block, four times the adaptive rule's 0.39, returns −0.0023.** The statistical name is endogeneity — the served difficulty is a function of the child's own earlier answers — and the mechanism is that `nextTargetTheta` aims at `theta0 + lambda * nextIndex`, so the path rises at the rate the running fit has estimated and a flat accuracy series over such a path is exactly the signature of an ability rising at that rate. The estimate is written into the design and read back out. **The first hypothesis is refuted, and refuted in the wrong direction. Conditioning the fit on served difficulty with a FREE coefficient — the shape of this project's own pre-registered Gate B statistic — roughly doubles the artifact, to +0.0167 on the grid and +0.0172 on the bank**, because difficulty was never omitted from the fit (it is already there as a known offset), and under adaptive targeting it is close to collinear with the trial index, so the extra freedom absorbs the signal. Both standard finite-sample bias corrections also move it UP and are rejected on measurement: the Jeffreys/Firth penalty gives +0.0133 (and +0.0314 under an exogenous schedule, because the penalty rewards parameter values carrying more Fisher information and on a rising path a larger `lambda` is what keeps the response probability off the chance floor), the delete-one jackknife +0.0132. **A conditional or profile likelihood has nothing to condition on: a selection rule depending only on the observed past is ignorable, so the shipped likelihood is already the right one and contains no missing term.** Correcting the floor "inside the likelihood" as a latent guess/know mixture is the same estimator algebraically — `P(correct) = c + (1−c)σ` is that mixture's marginal — so it was not implemented and the reason is stated rather than measured. **What works, and its price. `scheduledTargetTheta` administers from a schedule fixed before the block starts.** Gate A on all four banks and the grid, 8 seeds × 400 children, before → after: null λ̄ **grid 0.0085 → −0.0033, `FLU-OPCHAIN-01` 0.0097 → −0.0019, `SPA-XFORM-01` 0.0092 → −0.0021, `QUANT-GLYPHNUM-01` 0.0087 → −0.0027, `VER-MORPHO-01` 0.0166 → 0.0063**; the harness's own per-cell A1 verdict goes 3/8 → 7/8, 2/8 → **8/8**, 2/8 → **8/8**, 2/8 → 7/8, 0/8 → 3/8. **The change is a near-constant −0.011 on every pool (−0.0104 to −0.0118), which is what a single shared cause looks like.** `VER-MORPHO-01` keeps a residual because its items are four-option against a five-option estimator; correct that too and it joins the others at **−0.0024 ± 0.0009, per-cell A1 7/8**, so the floor defect and the design defect are separable and additive. **THE COST IS LARGER THAN THE BIAS REMOVED, and it lands on the only thing D-200 left reportable.** Mean posterior SE widens **0.062 → 0.079 on the grid and 0.063 → 0.085 on the two five-option banks, about a third**, so **A4 gets worse, not better** — the grid's per-cell A4 falls 2/8 → 0/8 and no pool passes it after. Because separability is tested against SE plus the declared contamination floor, and the half-width of the SD-0.15 reference is 0.075, **SE + floor moves from 0.071–0.073 (just inside) to 0.079–0.085 (outside), and the share of blocks where a band can be named at all falls 92% → 52–59%, or 64–72% → 52–58% with the floor declared — a 12 to 16 point loss on every pool whose floor was already correct.** Only `VER-MORPHO-01` gains (+22 points), because its 0.0166 floor was costing more than the widening. Recovery `r` is roughly unchanged at the design spread (grid 0.334 → 0.332, `QUANT-GLYPHNUM-01` 0.334 → 0.333) and falls about 0.023 on the two banks whose grids are sparser; at a wider true spread the loss grows (λ SD 0.10: 0.778 → 0.694). **A fast learner is the one who pays.** The schedule aims at a rate, not at a child, so a λ = 0.15 learner works the block at **64% correct under a 0.06 schedule and 73% under a flat one, against 49% under the adaptive rule** — items below their level, which is a ceiling for exactly the children R5 and R6 exist to measure. **A1's verdict also depends on which test is used, and both are reported: per-cell (the harness's rule, |λ̄| ≤ 2 Monte-Carlo SEs within one 400-child cell) passes; pooling eight seeds is roughly √8 more powerful and still places the remaining −0.002 to −0.003 about 2.5 SEs below zero.** A remedy that passed only the first would be passing by widening its own error bar. **The schedule's rate is not a knob tuned until a check passed, and the sweep is the proof: every rate from 0 to 0.30 returns the same null-cohort mean within error (−0.0036 to +0.0049) while recovery falls monotonically from 0.374 to 0.084 and mean SE rises from 0.070 to 0.131.** The rate buys nothing on A1 and costs on everything else; it is set to the population λ mean the harness already defaults to. | Verified measurement under known synthetic truth, in the one direction that is not circular: it measures what the pipeline produces from a child who did nothing, and it isolates the cause by holding the difficulty path fixed while changing only what chose it | `pnpm exam:block-harness -- --endogeneity-probe` produces the attribution and every rejected candidate in one table; `-- --gate-a --bank <ref> --targeting scheduled` runs Gate A under the remedy; `-- --gate-a --bank ideal-grid` runs the bank-free bound through the identical `gateA()` path rather than a separate probe. `node research/exam-question-types/lambda-endogeneity-gate-a.mjs` produces the full before/after across all four banks and the grid, and its header carries the read-only extraction commands for the three banks that live on other branches. Parameters as recorded on E-095 and E-200. Standing assertions in `packages/exam-scoring/src/learning-curve.test.ts` (`the manufactured climb is the loop, not the ladder` — three paired-cohort contrasts: the same paths replayed against independent answers lose the sign, a STEEPER exogenous ladder manufactures less, and the remedy widens the posterior; plus `scheduledTargetTheta`'s exogeneity, monotonicity and scale bounds). Decision D-206. Refines E-200's mechanism sentence, which attributed the climb to the fit reading "its own rising difficulty walk" — the walk is not what does it | The measurement is born-synthetic against `validated: false` banks with design-estimated difficulties, and none of it is evidence about a real child. The attribution rests on a simulated responder whose true ability really is flat, which is exactly the condition no real cohort can be known to satisfy. A schedule fixed before the block starts assumes the standing handover is accurate for the child (D-205's premise); where it is not, the whole block is mis-aimed with no mechanism to recover, and the adaptive rule's one real virtue is that